To balance seeking support from your partner with handling your anxiety on your own, consider implementing strategies that promote independence and self-regulation. One technique is to practice self-compassion and challenge negative self-talk by reframing anxious thoughts in a more realistic and positive light. Additionally, engage in activities that bring you a sense of calm and fulfillment, such as exercise, meditation, or hobbies, to help manage anxiety and build confidence.

Another approach is to establish a "worry time" each day, where you allocate a specific period to focus on your anxieties, and then set them aside for the rest of the day. This can help contain your worries and prevent them from spilling over into other areas of your life. It's also essential to communicate your needs and boundaries with your partner, ensuring that you're both on the same page regarding what you're comfortable with in terms of support and independence.

Building independence can also involve developing problem-solving skills and learning to tolerate uncertainty. This might involve taking small steps towards facing your fears or anxieties, and celebrating your successes along the way. By gradually building your resilience and self-efficacy, you can reduce your reliance on your partner for reassurance and develop a more balanced and healthy dynamic in your relationship.

